# 13. Uploading a File to S3

Boto3 provides the `upload_file()` method for uploading files.

### Syntax

```python
s3.upload_file(
    LOCAL_FILE,
    BUCKET_NAME,
    S3_OBJECT_NAME
)
```

### Example

```python
s3.upload_file(
    "test.txt",
    "my-first-boto3-bucket",
    "test.txt"
)
```

---

# 14. `upload_file()` Parameters

### Parameter 1 — Local File

```python
"test.txt"
```

The file located on our local computer.

```text
Kali
└── test.txt
```

---

### Parameter 2 — Bucket Name

```python
"my-first-boto3-bucket"
```

The S3 bucket where the object will be uploaded.

---

### Parameter 3 — S3 Object Name

```python
"test.txt"
```

The name/key that the object will have inside S3.

---

# 15. Upload Flow

```text
Kali Linux
    │
    │ test.txt
    ↓
  Boto3
    │
    ↓
   S3
    │
    ↓
 Bucket
    │
    ↓
 Object
 test.txt
```

---

# 16. Local Filename and S3 Object Name Can Be Different

They do not have to be the same.

Example:

```python
s3.upload_file(
    "test.txt",
    "my-first-boto3-bucket",
    "backup.txt"
)
```

Result:

```text
Kali
└── test.txt

        ↓ Upload

S3 Bucket
└── backup.txt
```

So:

```text
Local file = test.txt
S3 object  = backup.txt
```

